<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  EXPORT COMPLIANCE — owner determination 2026-08-04. Do not remove.

	  Without this key every upload lands in TestFlight as
	  MISSING_EXPORT_COMPLIANCE and cannot be installed by any tester until
	  the encryption and France questions are answered by hand in App Store
	  Connect. Build 48 sat blocked on exactly that.

	  The determination: all cryptography in this app is published-standard
	  and is used only to protect the user's own data on their own device and
	  to sign them in.
	    - SQLCipher 4.7.0 (AES-256) — database at rest
	    - scrypt, RFC 7914 — PIN hashing (AuthService.swift)
	    - PBKDF2 / SHA-256 — legacy PIN hashes, migrated on next login
	    - TLS — via the OS
	  None of it is proprietary cryptography. A hand-written implementation of
	  a published standard is still a published standard; "proprietary" means
	  an algorithm we invented, and we invented none.

	  This must live in the Info.plist. There is no INFOPLIST_KEY_ build
	  setting for it — an earlier attempt to set one that way (#1610) merged
	  as an empty commit and changed nothing, which is why the prompts kept
	  coming. If the crypto above ever changes, redo the determination with
	  the owner before touching this key.
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
	<key>NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription</key>
	<string>WiredPart uses Bluetooth to find and connect to nearby devices running WiredPart so you can add them to your company and sync data without a network.</string>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>WiredPart uses the camera to scan QR codes, barcodes, and documents for parts, tools, text recognition, document scanning, and device pairing.</string>
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>WiredPart connects to the shop computer and nearby devices on your local network to sync data.</string>
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>WiredPart records your location when you clock in/out and for mileage tracking on job sites.</string>
	<!--
	  NSBonjourServices is REQUIRED on iOS 14+ for the app to discover other
	  devices. Both the LAN sync (Network.framework NWBrowser, "_wiredpart._tcp")
	  and Bluetooth/Wi-Fi Multipeer (serviceType "wiredpart-sync" → Bonjour
	  "_wiredpart-sync._tcp"/"._udp") are silently blocked and return zero peers
	  unless their service types are listed here. Keep these in sync with:
	    core/Sources/WiredPartCore/Sync/PeerDiscovery.swift   (_wiredpart._tcp)
	    core/Sources/WiredPartCore/Sync/MultipeerManager.swift (wiredpart-sync)
	-->
	<key>NSBonjourServices</key>
	<array>
		<string>_wiredpart._tcp</string>
		<string>_wiredpart-sync._tcp</string>
		<string>_wiredpart-sync._udp</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
